<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Łyna</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Alle River</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Łyna, in German the Alle, is a river of about 290 kilometres that rises in the Masurian lake district of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> and flows north through Olsztyn and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lidzbark-warminski">Lidzbark Warmiński</a> into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaliningrad-oblast">Kaliningrad Oblast</a>, where it joins the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pregolya">Pregolya</a>. Its valley was settled by the Baltic Prussian Warmians and Barts and later formed the heart of Warmia under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teutonic-order">Teutonic Order</a> and the prince-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lyna.jpg" alt="Łyna" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: S.Czachorowski · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Las_warminski.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
